<TopicPage slug="subjects/horticulture/foundational/knowing-plants-classification-and-structure">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Horticulture begins with knowing plants precisely: their scientific names (which carry real
    information about needs and relatives), their structural anatomy (which explains how they grow
    and recover), and the classifications that predict care. The RHS Level 2 syllabus opens here
    for a practical reason — you cannot reliably grow, prune or propagate what you cannot identify.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="binomials, families, anatomy and the monocot split" widget="equationStep" config={namingCfg} />

  <h2>Why the scientific name earns its italics</h2>
  <p>
    "Daisy" names dozens of unrelated plants; Leucanthemum vulgare names exactly one, everywhere
    on Earth. Better: the genus groups genuinely related species — so knowing a plant's genus often
    predicts its soil preference, hardiness and how to propagate it. In horticulture the Latin is
    not pedantry; it is the database key.
  </p>

  <h2>The four organs and their jobs</h2>
  <p>
    Roots anchor, absorb water and minerals, and often store; stems hold leaves to the light and
    pipe water and sugars; leaves run photosynthesis; flowers run reproduction. Nearly every
    horticultural operation — watering, feeding, pruning, staking — intervenes in one of those
    four jobs, and the plant's response follows its anatomy.
  </p>

  <h2>Structure is why pruning works</h2>
  <p>
    Cut just above a bud facing outward and the new growth heads that way; cut into old wood on a
    plant that regrows from it, and the shrub rejuvenates; cut the cambium ring around the trunk
    and the plant dies. The cambium — the thin growth layer between bark and wood — is the
    gardener's real instrument, and respecting it is the first rule of the craft.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> RHS Level 2 syllabus (Principles of Plant Growth, Propagation and Development); Adams &amp; Bamford, <em>Principles of Horticulture</em>; Brickell, <em>RHS Encyclopedia of Plants and Flowers</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
